Two blockers up front, then everything you asked for.

**I couldn't write the file.** This session has no `Write`/`Edit`/`Bash` tool — only read/search tools. The test is below in full, ready to paste.

**Your checkout isn't here anyway.** `/private/tmp/e-yG8lw1/home/cwd` is empty: no `*.go` anywhere, no `internal/prwatch/summary.go`, zero hits for `HeadSHA`, and git shows no commits on `main`. So "the current directory" is not the head branch of `deividfortuna/babysitter` — everything below is reasoned from the snippet you pasted, not from the branch. `helper_test.go` also belongs in `internal/prwatch/`, not the repo root, or it won't see `Snapshot` or `summarize`.

## Verdict: right bug, shaky rationale — take the fix

The defect is real and needs no repo access to confirm. `HeadSHA` is `*string`, `summarize` dereferences it unconditionally, and a nil value panics with `invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference`. In a polling watch loop that unwinds the loop goroutine, so @mfs is also right about the blast radius.

Where I'd push back is the *cause*. A force-push doesn't null out the head — GitHub returns the PR with the new head SHA, so that specific story is probably wrong. Plausible nil paths are elsewhere: a deleted head branch or deleted fork on a cross-repo PR, a zero-value/cached `Snapshot` that never got populated, or a decode of a payload missing the field. That distinction matters because it changes what the fallback should say and whether anything upstream also needs fixing. I can't tell which path is live without the actual code.

None of that blocks the change. Nothing in the type guarantees non-nil, so the check is warranted on its own terms — just don't record "force-push causes nil head" as a known fact unless @mfs has a trace.

One adjacent thing worth a separate issue, not this PR: if a single bad snapshot can kill the watch loop, the loop wants a `recover`. Fixing this one deref only removes the one panic you know about.

## The failing test

`internal/prwatch/helper_test.go`:

```go
package prwatch

import (
	"strings"
	"testing"
)

func TestSummarizeNilHeadSHA(t *testing.T) {
	snap := &Snapshot{Title: "Add retry to watch loop"}

	defer func() {
		if r := recover(); r != nil {
			t.Fatalf("summarize panicked on nil HeadSHA: %v", r)
		}
	}()

	got := summarize(snap)

	if !strings.Contains(got, snap.Title) {
		t.Errorf("summarize(%+v) = %q, want it to contain the title", snap, got)
	}
	if strings.Contains(got, "%!") || strings.Contains(got, "0x") {
		t.Errorf("summarize(%+v) = %q, want a readable placeholder, not a formatting artifact", snap, got)
	}
}

func TestSummarizeIncludesHeadSHA(t *testing.T) {
	sha := "9f2c1ab"
	snap := &Snapshot{Title: "Add retry to watch loop", HeadSHA: &sha}

	got := summarize(snap)

	if !strings.Contains(got, sha) {
		t.Errorf("summarize(%+v) = %q, want it to contain %q", snap, got, sha)
	}
	if !strings.Contains(got, snap.Title) {
		t.Errorf("summarize(%+v) = %q, want it to contain the title", snap, got)
	}
}
```

The first test fails today via the recover-and-`Fatalf`. It deliberately does *not* pin an exact fallback string — that's your call when you write the fix. The second pins current behavior so the fix doesn't regress the normal path.

## Verify

